Why do we want to change?
=========================
Gitflow has a complex branching strucure, and is better suited for very large projects with
many developers and few yearly releases. The history is very hard to understand by looking at the repository.
The feature branch merge policy also makes the history more messy, and rebase should be preferred.

We are a small team doing regularly releases, we should really consider a more agile way of using git.

I think these articles exlains my reasoning pretty good:

https://georgestocker.com/2020/03/04/please-stop-recommending-git-flow/
https://www.endoflineblog.com/gitflow-considered-harmful
https://www.endoflineblog.com/oneflow-a-git-branching-model-and-workflow

Development
-----------
- `main` is the trunk branch. All new changes target `main` instead of
  `swp-develop`.
- To sync a feature branch with the latest changes, rebase on top of `main`
  rather than merging `main` into the feature branch. This keeps history linear
  and easy to read.

Example 1:
git checkout main
git pull main
git checkout my-feaure-branch
git rebase main

Example 2:
git checkout my-feature-branch
git pull main --rebase

- When a pull request is approved and ready to land, use rebase-merge (not a
  regular merge commit).


Releases
--------
- At the start of a release cycle, cut a protected release branch from the
  current HEAD of `main` (e.g. `release/1.3.0.18.0`).
- Bug fixes found during testing are pushed directly to the release branch.
- When the testing period is over, the final version is tagged and released.
- After release, the release branch is merged back into `main` so that all
  fixes are carried forward.


Patching releases
-----------------
- Patches are pushed to the relevant release branch, the new version is tagged,
  and a new release is cut.
- After patching, the release branch is merged back into `main`.
